WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.783
A small, steady input arrives at one point on a dendrite.

2
00:00:07.643 --> 00:00:09.823
How much of it survives the trip along the branch?

3
00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:15.467
A dendrite is a thin tube of salty cytoplasm, wrapped in leaky membrane.

4
00:00:16.517 --> 00:00:18.997
Current injected at one point has two paths.

5
00:00:19.837 --> 00:00:22.397
It can flow along the core, or out through the membrane.

6
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:25.770
Think of a garden hose full of small holes.

7
00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:28.310
Some water leaks out at every hole.

8
00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:31.923
So the farther along you go, the less is left.

9
00:00:32.883 --> 00:00:35.863
How far the current gets depends on which path is easier.

10
00:00:36.723 --> 00:00:38.083
A wide core carries it farther.

11
00:00:38.983 --> 00:00:40.223
A tight wall loses less.

12
00:00:41.266 --> 00:00:44.566
The length constant puts that contest into one number.

13
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:50.430
It is the length of cable whose core resistance equals the resistance of its own

14
00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:51.430
membrane.

15
00:00:51.883 --> 00:00:56.903
Along a long, uniform cable, a steady signal falls exponentially with distance.

16
00:00:58.016 --> 00:01:01.776
So, one length constant away from the source, is the signal gone?

17
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:11.130
No. About 37% percent remains.

18
00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:12.850
Smaller, not gone.

19
00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:17.403
Two length constants away, about 14% % remains.

20
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:20.923
The signal keeps shrinking, but it never simply stops.

21
00:01:21.983 --> 00:01:23.983
Here is the lesson's worked example.

22
00:01:24.903 --> 00:01:28.263
Watch the steady signal shrink at each length constant along the cable.

23
00:01:29.283 --> 00:01:31.963
The length constant sets the scale of the decay.

24
00:01:32.863 --> 00:01:33.863
It is not a cutoff.

25
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:36.783
A leakier membrane shortens it.

26
00:01:37.383 --> 00:01:38.523
So does a narrower core.

27
00:01:39.516 --> 00:01:41.316
Try it in the lesson's simulation.

28
00:01:42.096 --> 00:01:46.576
Make the membrane leakier, and watch the 37% % point move toward the source.

29
00:01:47.616 --> 00:01:52.676
With the same membrane and cytoplasm, the length constant grows as the square root

30
00:01:52.676 --> 00:01:53.676
of the diameter.

31
00:01:54.416 --> 00:01:56.476
Capacitance does not change the shape.

32
00:01:57.316 --> 00:01:59.536
It only sets how fast the steady state arrives.

33
00:02:00.566 --> 00:02:03.286
Now double the leak, with everything else the same.

34
00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.950
In a compact cell, input resistance halves.

35
00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:09.430
The same current gives half the change.

36
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.700
The time constant halves too, so the voltage gets there sooner.

37
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.590
And along the cable, the length constant shrinks.

38
00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:19.450
Signals reach less far.

39
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.360
Does the passive balance point move?

40
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.580
That depends on the added leak's reversal potential.

41
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:31.180
With no applied current, and the pump's own current set aside, doubling every leak

42
00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.880
in proportion leaves it unchanged.

43
00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:35.833
More leak makes a cell faster.

44
00:02:36.413 --> 00:02:39.413
But its responses are smaller, and they reach less far.
